EM: Well, It was full of people from the whole apartment. They went down to the shelter, too.

INT: So how did they get food?

EM: I don't know. I was too young to know that.

INT: How long did you have to remain in the basement?

EM: It all depends how much the attack was on-- an hour, two hours, half an hours. It all depends. It was a bomb attack.

INT: And what kind of damage was done?

EM: In the buildings? Many of them was damaged.

INT: And where you lived, was it damaged as well?

EM: No, not in that place, thank God.

INT: And tell me about a day-to-day survival in the ghetto.

EM: Well, we have the Jewish organization. They provides sometimes food. But we have to go there to get it. It all depends when it was and what it was. Sometimes, it was soup. Sometimes, it was vegetable, bread. This all depends. We have to go get it.

INT: Where?

EM: In the Jewish organization.

INT: But where did you have to go?

EM: In the ghetto. It was in the ghetto.

INT: So a special place in the ghetto?

EM: Yes. It was a special place, yes. That was like a-- it was a Jewish organization. And-- but it was a few-- go a few blocks away from my house to there. And you have to make sure, you are going there, there is no alarm for the attack. You are not going to be on the street that time when is attack is coming. But this was very hard to know it by heart. When it-- the alarm went on, then we know that it's coming.

INT: Where was the ghetto located?

EM: In the Seventh District in Budapest, in Pest.

INT: In Pest. And where was the Seventh District?

EM: In the middle of the city.

INT: In the middle of the city. How many--

EM: There was 14 different districts. We was in the seventh.

INT: And how big was the ghetto area?

EM: Large. I don't know by heart by meter or miles, but it was a large-- it was from one avenue to the other avenue. But it was a long way. It was a large, large place.

INT: When you say avenue, did the avenue have a name?

EM: Yes, korút. And [PAUSES 5 SECONDS] the other one is somebody's name, Bajcsy-Zsilinszky út. I don't know by English-- somebody's name.

INT: And you said 17,000 people?

EM: I didn't say that.

INT: Oh, I thought you said 17.

EM: I said it was 14 district in Budapest.

INT: No, no, the people. How many people were in the ghetto?

EM: I don't know, several thousand.

