EM: Well, it was set by time, when I was able to go on the street, between certain hours in the day. And after, I have to go back to my place and not to appear on the street.

INT: What hours were they?

EM: I don't remember the hours. It was different. It was from the morning till the afternoon. I don't know by heart. And [PAUSES 7 SECONDS] so I was limited to go to the streets on a certain time.

INT: How did they know you were Jewish?

EM: We have to wear the Jewish star on my [GESTURING] clothes.

INT: And how was the ghetto established? How would-- how did they decide which houses or which apartment building?

EM: Well, it was boarded in, in certain area, certain street. Actually, my building where I was living, it was next door to the board for the closing-- [PAUSES] it was the border of the-- between-- on the street, closing up the street with the board. And that was the closing in my place. There were several different places. It was four different area. It was South, West, East.

INT: And it was boarded up with boards?

EM: Yes.

INT: So you couldn't get out?

EM: That's right. There was a door on it on each board.

INT: And at what time or at what point were you allowed to get out of there?

EM: When we was in the ghetto-- [COUGHING] when we was in the ghetto, we didn't have no limit of going on the street. When we was in the Jewish building, then when we have a limited time.

INT: Tell me about the Jewish building.

EM: The Jewish building, I moved in with my grandparents. My father was in a Jewish work camp. So we didn't know him. We didn't hear from him. And I went with my mother. I have friends in the building. I have two girlfriends and one boyfriends. And we got together to socializing. And [PAUSES] my two aunt was home until they took her to the concentration camp.

