EM: Because they want us together. The Jewish people-- they want the Jewish people together in one place.

INT: But wasn't the ghetto one place for you to be together?

EM: Yes. They want to get-- therefore, they want us to leave the large building. And the Jewish people have to leave this building to go into the ghetto. It was a order from the government.

INT: So the Jewish buildings came first, and then they took you to the ghetto?

EM: Yes. Yes.

INT: How long were you in the Jewish buildings?

EM: I don't remember, several months-- maybe for close to a year or so. I don't remember exactly.

INT: Do-- what month did you have to go there? What month did they take you to the building?

EM: I don't remember the date.

INT: How long were you there?

EM: I don't know, several months.

INT: And this was in 1944?

EM: Yes.

INT: When you were in the ghetto, how rampant was disease?

EM: I don't remember any.

INT: And no one was sick?

EM: Not that I know of.

INT: How hungry were you?

EM: Many times, I was hungry, many times. But my mother was providing everything which was possible to go to the Jewish organization to get food, what leftover.

INT: Do you know who guarded the ghetto?

EM: No. Oh, who guarded the ghetto?

The Hungarian Nazis. I don't know how you call that in English.

INT: In Hungarian?

EM: Nazi. [PAUSES 5 SECONDS] Nyilasok, Nyilasok.

INT: What did you know about the Arrow Cross? Arrow Cross, the Hungarian Nazis. What did you know about them?

EM: Well, they hate Jewish. And they was torturing them, if they have a chance, and bring them to work for the government.

INT: Did you witness any brutality? Or what brutality did you witness?

EM: I witness. My apartment windows was looking down to the street near to the border of the ghetto wall. And opposite to the building was standing the Nyilas. And they took-- when the Hungarian Jewish people went by, they tear down the gold earrings from their ears. And they took all the jewelry off from them. And that's what I was witnessing. After, when the Russian came in and free us, we was liberated by the Russian. Also, opposite to my building was a drugstore and was a-- [PAUSES 5 SECONDS] anyway, there was a drugstore. And they open up the store. And they was putting the toothpaste on the bread. They spread the toothpaste on the bread. And they drink the cologne. They didn't know better. They was very low-educated people, that Russian people who was liberating us. Yeah.

INT: Tell me about the day that you were liberated.

EM: It was 1945 January-- February or January, I don't know exactly. And I remember then, one of the Hungarian-- [PAUSES 8 SECONDS] one of the Hungarian people came to my apartment and brought two slices of bread spread with [PAUSES] pork grease. And I never have a better meal in my life. It was-- tasted so good after I was starving from food. Yeah.

INT: How did your stomach react to that?

EM: Fine. Took it fine.

INT: At any time, did you have-- were you able to hide from these deportations that were taking place?

EM: Oh, yes. When we was in the Jewish appointed people building-- there was a star on the door. When they was collecting the Jewish people, in the garden, in the middle. And they got together. And we was missing with-- my mother and I was missing. And one of the Nazis was yelling at. We're not going to leave without-- the Mrs. Schwarcz with the 12 year old daughter is missing. We're not going to leave without them. But they have to, because we was hiding, in the shelter, in the basement. And we was locked in. My mother had that time a pneumonia. And she had to cough. And she couldn't cough, because she doesn't want to make a noise. And the group was leaving, at afternoon, without my mother and I. And the basement was locked up on us. And one of the men-- after the group was leaving, many hours after that, one of the men came to open the basement door. And we was escaping. So we was hiding there. They couldn't wait till I appear again.

INT: How did they know you were missing?

EM: Because they know that the people who was living in the-- there was a-- [PAUSES 5 SECONDS] I still don't know the name of the people who was handling the building, the people-- I don't know how you call these people.

INT: Maybe bookkeeper or something like that?

EM: Not bookkeeper, no. They was managing the building-- the manager of the building.

INT: Had everybody's name?

EM: Yes, provide them, too, their names and the apartment number.

INT: And did you have a roll call? Is that how they-- a roll call? Did they call everybody's name to get--

EM: Oh, yes, they did. And I was missing. And my mother was missing too, yes.

INT: So how did you know where to hide in the ghetto? How did you know where to go? You said you went to the basement.

EM: In the Jewish building?

INT: How did you know to go there?

EM: Well, the common sense. It was nowhere else to hide.

INT: And you were in the ghetto for how long, did you say?

EM: From June till February-- June, July, August, September, October, November, December, January, February-- about eight months.

INT: What did you know about what was happening outside, either in Hungary or in Poland or in Czechoslovakia?

EM: I didn't know nothing.

INT: You didn't know anything what was happening to the other Jewish people?

EM: No. We don't have no communication, [PAUSES] no news program what tell the truth.

