INT: And so the day that you were liberated, tell me about that day.

EM: I-- clearly, I don't remember, just I know what--

INT: Where did you go?

EM: I stayed home, I think so, but I don't remember.

INT: Were you able to go back to your original apartment?

EM: Well, I was in my original apartment. I took people from the Jewish building. 17 people, I took it with us. And we was living in our apartment, yes.

INT: Was anybody else living there that took over your apartment when you were--

EM: 17 other people.

INT: No, other people-- not the people who were in the ghetto, but, like, your neighbors, did they take your apartment over?

EM: No.

INT: They left it empty for you?

EM: Yeah.

INT: OK. And now, it's January.

EM: January or February. I don't remember. I think it was February.

INT: Cold winter? Cold winter?

EM: Very cold winter-- snow and-- it was big snow pile on the streets.

INT: And tell me about the people that didn't survive.

