INT: Taken from the hospital?

EM: Yes, from the building where I was in the annex people, yeah, annex building.

INT: How did that affect you?

EM: Badly. Badly. That is always in my memory. It can't cleared up.

INT: And what did they do with the bodies at that time?

EM: Until they was able to get a horse to move away the buggies, it was still there for a few days or months or weeks. I don't know. That I don't remember.

INT: About how many bodies would you say, just give me an approximate number.

EM: It's about 30. [PAUSES] It was logs, back and forth, head and feet.

INT: How did the Soviet soldiers treat the local people?

EM: They didn't select them. Whoever was on the street, most of the mens, come rabotnik, rabotnik. This means, come to work. They took them. We didn't know that's where they took them, just to rabotnik. This means "working."

INT: And the women?

EM: Well, they assault the women, too. But I don't know personally the person what it was. They was collecting chasy. Chasy means wristwatch. And-- [PAUSES] what were the chasy and? [PAUSES] I know they was after the chasy. But it was something else, too. But I don't remember. I know it was something else. [PAUSES] They was very badly manner-- very bad manners, no education, very low-class people. I mean, the soldiers, I'm talking about.

INT: So now, it's 1945, you are 13, 14 years old.

EM: 13.

INT: What were your goals at that time?

EM: What was?

INT: What were your goals? What were your desires?

EM: Well, I was finishing school, high school. And after, I went to learn a trade. I was a seamstress. I was learning the the profession from one of my aunts-- was a very good professional. She used to have a salon. And--

INT: And you said your mother, too, had golden hands.

EM: Oh, yes. Yes. She did.

INT: Did she did help you as well?

EM: Not really, not in this case, no. She doesn't have to, because I have my aunt who helped me out. And also, there was an other woman who was recommend me. I also went to learn with her, too. So when I came to this country, I went to the factory, actually. But I didn't know the work, because that was quantity. And I was used to make custom-made. And it's a big difference between. And they said, in America, time is money. They don't have no time to teach me for the quantity of work. And I have 11 job in the first year. And I never have collected the [PAUSES 5 SECONDS] unemployment, because I didn't have enough time in one place. So I didn't collect no money.

INT: What year did you emigrate to the United States?

EM: 1957, January.

INT: How did you get here?

EM: I came with a army boat from Bremerhaven to New Jersey. And we arrived to Camp Kilmer. I don't know where is it in Jersey because I never saw that. Only when I was arriving to this country, we came to Camp Kilmer, New Jersey.

INT: What was the name of the ship?

EM: I don't know the name of the ship, but it was a army ship. We was there about 120 refugees on the ship. And we came on January on the sea. It was very, very high wind. And the water was going over of the nose of the ship. And the men's bathroom was in the nose in the ship. And they was having very bad time. They couldn't shave, because the boat was constantly jumping up and down. And I remember, my bathroom didn't have no-- in each stall, they didn't have no doors. They have canvas curtains. And we was vomiting from the jumping up of the ship. And when somebody was in the opposite side, we-- the back of us was meeting when we were leaning down over the toilet. It was not nice.

INT: How long did the journey take?

EM: 11 days.

INT: And were you sick the whole time?

