INT: What was happening with the government in Hungary before you left?

EM: Well, they was under the Russian--

INT: Occupation?

EM: --occupation, yes.

INT: And so did you feel free at that time?

EM: No.

INT: Because?

EM: Because we was under the Russian regulation, communism. And you wasn't free speech and different kind of regulation what I don't remember. But we was tricked by them. And--

INT: How did you feel about communism?

EM: I wasn't agree with it. It was Stalin that time, if you remember his name-- and Lenin and Stalin. And they want to have the communism organization. They have a communist parties. And some of them was-- some people was agree with them. So they went to the Communist Party. And it was Socialist Party, too. And I wasn't a member of the organization because I wasn't even agree with none of them. And I was young anyhow.

INT: So you had the desire to leave?

EM: Oh, yes, definitely. And I left in November on 26, I believe so, if I remember well. And we went to-- with a big truck. It was about five or six people together. They want to do the same thing, to escape from Hungary. And we was lucky enough to do so.

INT: When you got to the United States, you were still speaking Hungarian?

EM: Yes.

INT: How did you learn how to speak English?

